text:
Too many reviews have covered the bases and probably more eloquently than I.In short;
Does it look pretty? Yes, stunningly so.Is that enough in itself? NoThe story is bland and highly repetitive to the first film with more plot holes than a Swiss cheese (don't get me started on Colonel Miles Quaritch's return). There are large drops in frame rates when there's too much CGI.Eight of us (all fans of the first film) went to see it and we spent longer than the film itself discussing all the "issues" we had with it afterwards.Watch it? Sure, why not.Revisit it? No
Eagerly await the next one? Not a chance.

Someone on a forum many years ago had in their signature: "Those who write poorly have good commentators; those who write well have good listeners."
Avatar: Fire and Ash (2025) struck me as a film that gave me ideas and emotions.Dune: Part Two (2024) is a very good film; I gave it a 10. But it didn't give me many emotions.The newest film in the Avatar series is much weaker in terms of the screenplay, but despite that, I felt good watching it and it challenged me. As a film with good commentators, it can sometimes be more interesting to watch weaker films as well.I find it hard to analyze it. At school we were told there are two ways to analyze something:
a. You can start from the maximum score and subtract points whenever an error appears;
b. Or you can start from the assumption that you're analyzing something of low quality and add a positive point for each successful element.If I were to start from the maximum score and subtract for every logical error, there wouldn't be much left of the grade.The screenplay has so many errors and blunders that you quickly end up with very low scores.I'll give just one example-you have to shoot at a huge bulldozer, with an absolutely massive front bucket. It seems obviously stupid to shoot at that bucket, several centimeters thick and made of metal. In the film, people shoot at the bucket.It's just one example, but the film can irritate you if you start from the premise that it's a good film. It has so many slip-ups that it becomes annoying.If you start from the premise that it's a weak point, I can find two good things about it:
a. Visually, it works;
b. It has a few strong moments.There are a few more positive aspects:
a. One of the negative characters is fairly well outlined, but nothing spectacular;
b. Action from start to finish. There are moments without fights or chases, but something is constantly happening on screen;
c. It keeps you engaged, it has suspense-something some action films tend to lose;
d. In the first two films, the spirituality aspect was more like "yes, it matters, the characters emphasize it, but the impact is minimal"; finally, it seemed to me that the spiritual aspect plays an important role.My rating is 8.5. It can be seen in the cinema, it's OK, but it's not a WOW film, from my perspective. The screenplay can get on your nerves at times; it can be very annoying.The fact that it's very long doesn't detract from the atmosphere-I liked it, it helped me disconnect.
